@prefix ag:          <http://example.org/agora#> .
@prefix rdfs:        <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:         <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix calibration: <http://example.org/agora/calibration#> .

<http://example.org/agora/calibration> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Agora calibrations" ;
    rdfs:comment """How a raw value becomes a QUANTITY — the last of the three stages a reading passes through. Two things happen here and only one of them is arithmetic: a raw number is SCALED by whatever curve this device follows, and the result is given a UNIT. The second is easy to forget and bites first, because a board reporting several properties puts a soil moisture of 0.183 and an air humidity of 0.46 in the same store looking identical while meaning different things. A family borne by a BINDING rather than by an agent, so a member is declared on the sensor and chosen at runtime; nothing about an agent is derived from it. See knowledge/decisions/bytes-become-a-quantity-in-stages.md.""" .

#################  The family  #################

calibration:Calibration a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Calibration" ;
    rdfs:comment "One way of turning what a device reported into what it means. A family because a curve is per-DEVICE and not per-model: two probes of one part number in different soil do not share one, which is the test for a family rather than a constant." .

calibration:calibratedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range calibration:Calibration ;
    rdfs:label "calibrated by" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which curve turns this device's raw values into quantities. ABSENT means calibration:Identity, which is what every sensor here relies on today. A stated calibration is answered only by the member whose term it is, so it can never be overruled by the default." .

#################  The unit  #################

calibration:quantityUnit a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "quantity unit" ;
    rdfs:comment """What unit the quantity coming out of the calibration is in. The object is a QUDT unit IRI — `unit:DEG_C`, `unit:UNITLESS` — because SOSA deliberately defines no units and names QUDT as one of the vocabularies to reach for, so this is the standard companion to the vocabulary already in use here.

BORROWED, NOT IMPORTED, exactly as settlement-speaks-rea decided for ValueFlows: the IRIs are referenced and nothing of QUDT is loaded, so `agent/inference.py`'s hand-materialised closure gains no axioms to cover. `unit:` is declared in the kernel's prefix list rather than here, because an external vocabulary is not a package's to bind — a package that could rebind it could quietly redirect every unit in the society.

Stating this converts nothing and is not meant to. Every number in the store is exactly what it was; what changes is that `0.183` now says it is a dimensionless fraction and `21.4` says it is degrees Celsius, where before the distinction lived in prose. That is what makes issue #26 a conversion between STATED units rather than a guess.""" .

#################  The members  #################

calibration:Identity a calibration:Calibration ; rdfs:label "Identity" ;
    rdfs:comment "The quantity is the raw value. The default, the only implemented member, and NOT a placeholder: the firmware scales counts to a fraction before it publishes, so from the agent's side the calibration genuinely is the identity function. The stage is not missing here — it is set to identity, which is a different and honest thing to say. What its sensor still owes is the UNIT: identity does not mean dimensionless." .

calibration:Linear a calibration:Calibration ; rdfs:label "Linear" ;
    rdfs:comment "Declared, unimplemented. A gain and an offset — the ordinary answer for an analogue probe read through an ADC, and what issue #26 would need first. Its arrival is the interesting moment rather than the code: the moment a curve lives in the agent instead of compiled into C, it stops being a constant and becomes a BELIEF, which is a thing an agent may hold within stated bounds and re-pick as a probe drifts. That is why moving this work off the board is worth more than the arithmetic suggests." .

calibration:TwoPoint a calibration:Calibration ; rdfs:label "Two-point" ;
    rdfs:comment "Declared, unimplemented. Calibration from two measured references — dry air and water, for a soil probe — rather than from a gain and an offset someone wrote down. The standard field procedure, and the one that makes recalibration something an agent could be walked through rather than a firmware change." .
